content.text:
Here's the CSS so far, in detail. Then I'll keep moving on step 2.

### `_variables.scss` — tokens only, emits no CSS

Plain SCSS variables (`$name: value`). Nothing here produces output; it exists so that a colour or size is written down once. Three things worth checking:

- **`$breakpoints` is a SCSS map** — `('lg': 1200px, 'md': 992px, ...)`. Maps are key→value lookups, consumed by `map.get()` in the mixin below. This is what lets me write `@include respond-to('sm')` instead of repeating `@media (max-width: 768px)` in thirty places.
- **`$shadow-soft` uses `rgba($navy-deep, 0.12)`** — SCSS's `rgba()` takes a colour *variable* plus an alpha and resolves it at compile time to a literal `rgba(13, 31, 61, 0.12)`. Plain CSS `rgba()` can't take a variable like that; this is a SASS function shadowing the CSS one.
- **`$nav-height` / `$nav-height-shrunk`** are shared between the navbar's own height and the `scroll-margin-top` that keeps section headings clear of it. One number, two consumers — that's the bug this prevents.

### `_mixins.scss` — the four reusable blocks

```scss
@mixin respond-to($name) {
    $width: map.get(v.$breakpoints, $name);
    @if $width == null { @error 'Unknown breakpoint: #{$name}'; }
    @media (max-width: $width) { @content; }
}
```

- `@use 'sass:map'` pulls in SASS's built-in map module — needed for `map.get`.
- `@use 'variables' as v` namespaces that file, so every reference reads `v.$breakpoints`. This is the modern replacement for `@import`: a file is loaded **once** no matter how many partials `@use` it, and the `v.` prefix makes it obvious where a value came from. `@import` would dump everything into one global soup and re-evaluate on each import.
- `@error` makes a typo (`respond-to('small')`) fail the build with a message instead of silently emitting `@media (max-width: null)`.
- `@content` is the placeholder where the block passed to the mixin gets injected.

`overlay($color, $opacity)` is the one non-obvious mixin:

```scss
position: relative;
&::before { content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; background-color: rgba($color, $opacity); }
> * { position: relative; }
```

It paints a tinted sheet over a background image so white text stays legible. `inset: 0` is shorthand for all four of `top/right/bottom/left: 0`. The `> *` rule is the part that matters: giving the real children `position: relative` puts them in the same stacking context as the absolutely-positioned `::before`, and since they come later in paint order they land **on top** of the tint rather than under it. No `z-index` needed. `> *` matches elements only, so it doesn't fight with `::before` itself.

### `_base.scss` — the shared layer

- `*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box }` — padding and borders count *inside* a declared width. Without it every width calculation in the responsive pass would be off by the padding.
- Margins zeroed on `h1/h2/h3`, `p`, `ul`. Browser default margins collapse unpredictably between siblings; I'd rather all vertical rhythm come from explicit spacing on containers.
- `.stripe` is the full-width horizontal band from requirement 1 — shared vertical padding, and `overflow: hidden` so the Guarantee marquee can't push out a horizontal scrollbar later.
- `.container` is the centring half: `max-width: 72rem; margin: 0 auto`. That's requirement 8's horizontal centring, applied once rather than per section.
- **`.icon { width: 1em; height: 1em; fill: currentColor }`** — this is the whole scalable-vector-icon mechanism. `1em` means the icon scales with whatever font-size its parent has, and `currentColor` means it inherits the text colour, so hover states apply to the icon for free without touching `fill`.

Now onto the navbar and hero.

